Share model data with future interpreters

Introduce runtime signatures and dynamic structures so callers can
inspect and construct Eqlog data without generating Rust or running
saturation. Keep old/new partitions, union-find state, and membership
relations in the same storage types used by compiled models. Select
relation arities and column orders at runtime.

Preserve element IDs, equality aliases, and raw relation rows through
conversion. Share prefix-tree nodes where index layouts agree and copy
union-find vectors deeply. Rebuild compiled lookup indices on import
without resolving pending equalities or executing rules.

Test incomplete structures, descriptor validation, column permutations,
copy independence, and continued evaluation after conversion.
